\newcommand{\ResultsFigureDirectory}{../../reports/results/tfg_shared_modes/figures}
\newcommand{\PrimarySeeds}{24}
\newcommand{\AblationSeeds}{12}
\newcommand{\ScenarioCount}{27}
\newcommand{\AbstractResults}{The condition-balanced TFG change relative to shared QMEKF is +0.50\% (95\% CI +0.30 to +0.69\%) for heave and -1.43\% (95\% CI -2.02 to -0.84\%) for horizontal position. There are 0 flagged estimator trajectories across all registered operational and diagnostic cells. However, reduced heave causes RMS heading above 30 degrees in 24/24 shared-QMEKF and 17/24 shared-TFG runs, despite no divergence/nonfinite flag. Real-only coupling removes this heading failure in a separate 12-seed diagnosis.}
\newcommand{\RegressionResults}{The accompanying regression record lists the original and post-refactor gates and exact paired numerical differences. Existing thresholds are retained. The full-wrapper coupling-off smoke uses the existing TFG/CARMA 1\% agreement criterion; the process-law identities use $10^{-11}$ relative tolerance.}
\newcommand{\ResultsDiscussion}{Condition-balanced heave changes by +0.50\% (95\% CI +0.30 to +0.69\%) and horizontal position by -1.43\% (95\% CI -2.02 to -0.84\%) when replacing shared QMEKF with shared TFG. Only 1 scenario/endpoints meet both the predeclared 5\% improvement margin and the Holm significance criterion; 3 show statistically supported worsening. The complete per-condition table prevents small aggregate changes from hiding different behavior in weak excitation, maneuvers or outages. The interaction table and figure evaluate nonadditivity directly. There are 0 flagged trajectories; their identities and the remaining consistency defects are retained below. Exact conditional-prior algebra does not make the inherited wave/bias/attitude approximation statistically calibrated. In the nominal case, joint physical NEES is 39.9 for shared TFG and 40.5 for shared QMEKF, against a 9-state reference mean of 9. This diagnostic is not repaired by the geometry change.}
\newcommand{\PerformanceResults}{On this host, the median of three repetitions is 7.99 us per 200 Hz core step for shared TFG, versus 3.98 us for shared QMEKF (ratio 2.01). With 25\% interval jitter the corresponding times are 10.09 and 5.97 us. Timings include 0.1 s model commits and magnetic updates at 25 Hz; percentiles and maxima are reported separately, and host scheduling can affect their tails.}
\newcommand{\FirmwareResults}{The Xtensa probe gives 14472 bytes for the shared TFG core versus 12504 for shared QMEKF. Their largest emitted function stack frames are 1744 and 1888 bytes; the static update-chain lower bounds are 3664 and 4864 bytes. The compiler used for this probe is Espressif GCC esp-14.2.0\_20241119 with \texttt{-Os}. A full Arduino firmware build is recorded separately in the reproducibility bundle. With matched Arduino optimization flags, the complete AtomS3R shared-mode application links at 1,029,687 bytes for TFG and 1,094,467 bytes for QMEKF; both use 31,660 bytes of globals. These figures exclude the estimator allocated at construction, whose core size is tabulated separately. The board package is ESP32 3.3.7 with M5Unified 0.2.13 and Espressif GCC 14.2.0\_20251107. The 200 Hz measurement harness also compiles, but its serial timing/heap/stack results require a connected board.}
\newcommand{\VerificationResults}{Measured maximum prediction finite-difference discrepancy is $3.23\times10^{-10}$; the relative independent continuous-covariance discrepancy is $6.27\times10^{-11}$. The anisotropic model-coordinate covariance discrepancy is $8.52\times10^{-11}$. Allocation-guarded float execution explicitly forces a falling-confidence fallback and remains finite with symmetric covariance; round-off eigenvalues are recorded rather than hidden.}
\newcommand{\Recommendation}{Do not replace shared QMEKF generally with shared TFG. The condition-balanced position changes are small, the added host cost is substantial, and the only greater-than-5\% supported position gain occurs inside a severe reduced-heave failure of both shared filters. Retain the reusable TFG implementation as an experimental research vehicle. Further accuracy work should first investigate quadrature coupling, confidence and attitude/bias consistency under reduced heave; the independent-axis and real-only diagnostics are useful controls, not validated operational replacements. Full reset transport remains the mathematically verified default. Actual ESP32 timing and stack high-water measurements are still required. The shipping OU-III default remains unchanged.}
\newcommand{\FailureDiscussion}{Reduced heave is the decisive adverse case. Mean heave/horizontal errors are 21.8/163.2 mm for shared QMEKF and 25.0/131.4 mm for shared TFG; independent TFG achieves 5.4/23.4 mm. Shared heading RMS averages 95.2 and 70.9 degrees. The apparent practically large horizontal geometry gain is therefore a comparison of two poor estimators, accompanied by worse heave. In the separate exploratory diagnosis, real-only coupling lowers heading RMS to 2.02 and 1.89 degrees with zero 30-degree exceedances. Coupling-off results also return to the independent-axis reference. Known quadrature coupling still produces severe heading error, so this cannot be attributed solely to online coupling-estimation error. Confidence falls and invokes the PSD fallback, but does not prevent the failure. This implicates quadrature-prior interaction with the weak-heave attitude/bias ambiguity; it is an experimental diagnosis, not a proof of a unique causal mechanism. No operational prior was retuned after observing these outcomes.}
\newcommand{\SupplementDiscussion}{The pinned eight-record replay gives heave changes between -0.0127\% and -0.0007\%, too small to justify a geometry switch. These intervals condition on each wave record and resample only sensors/initialization. The 24-seed full-rate transition supplement finds no practically meaningful recovery advantage; 0 transition recoveries are censored. Its exported positions have 10-micrometre resolution, so sub-resolution differences are not evidence for useful improvement. The six-condition reset supplement shows small ordinary-case position changes for both geometries; in reduced heave, omitting reset transport changes the failure mode substantially rather than fixing the prior. Reversed unchecked coupling degrades horizontal error by 7.55\% in rapid rotations, while confidence reduces that diagnostic to the uncoupled response. Confidence is useful but does not guarantee robustness.}
\newcommand{\SecondaryDiscussion}{Secondary attitude and bias results are condition dependent. At 800 rpm, shared TFG reduces mean tilt RMS from 0.45 to 0.31 degrees, heading from 3.02 to 2.10 degrees, and accelerometer-bias error from 7.84 to 5.40 mg. Under colored noise, heading also improves from 3.73 to 2.47 degrees while horizontal position worsens. Conversely, maneuvering worsens heading from 1.75 to 2.28 degrees: paired absolute change +0.53 degrees, marginal 95\% CI [+0.37, +0.68]. Gyro-bias RMS also worsens there. These secondary intervals are exploratory and are not part of the Holm-controlled primary position family. They motivate focused vibration/attitude research, not a claim of a general TFG accuracy gain.}
